eturn to the original question in  terms of mixed messaging  we have been told a variety of things  that now seem rather shaky for example  the idea that this pathway out of  lockdown is is irreversible  and boris johnson said back in downing  street and until end of february we will  not go back  we are now traveling on a one-way road  to freedom i mean that that's wrong  isn't it that's just simply not correct  we could see restrictions being  reapplied later on in the year  well i think the the message has been  very clear throughout which is the  vaccine is the key people need to get  their jobs  people have responsibilities let's clear  this up is it reversible or not well we  have taken the cautious of the prime  minister addressed this at the press  conference yesterday we've been cautious  in order uh to seek for it to be  irreversible but one never knows  uh you know what he did back in february  when he said we will not go back we are  now traveling  the vaccine has provided that wall an  extra seven million  uh vaccines have been delivered as a  result of the extra four weeks  and that's why we've taken that extra  time but we need to learn to live  with the virus we need to get those  businesses open some business has been  closed  for 16 months so it's right we get the  economy moving in that way  and learn to live with the virus but the  key throughout and our message has been  consistent  that the vaccine is key your viewers  have responded  that's perhaps the one thing the  government has been consistent on that  the vaccine is key to all of this let's  talk about vaccine passports again  nadeem sahibwi back in february we're  not planning to have a passport in the  uk  michael gove on this program will i need  one to go to large sporting events no  passports that's not being planned and  yesterday we again hear from the prime  minister backtracking  that these businesses you know perhaps  which will have large crowds within them  should be using them as a matter of  social responsibility  well i mean if you're saying is this  unprecedented is this complex  is this challenging has the government  have our scientists  been learning about the virus as we went  through we saw that before christmas  when the kent variant came along we had  to change our plans of course we  followed the data  and we've changed our plans if the data  has required that  we've followed the science throughout  but it's been complex it's been  unprecedented i think your viewers  appreciate that but they also recognize  that the vaccine is the way through this  we have deployed that two-thirds of the  adult population now having their two  jobs  um so the vaccine is the way forward but  we also need to live with  this condition and and get the economy  moving again sure  i will note however in the space of  about a week and a half the emphasis  being placed in all of this by the  government has changed entirely from you  know  ministers referring to it as freedom day  on this program  it's now softly softly be as cautious as  we can i mean just in terms of masks  and we had robert generic on the fourth  of july saying people need to take  personal responsibility  you had your boss at the treasury saying  that he was going to stop wearing them  as soon as the requirement was  was dropped now we're being told that  being wearing masks is to be encouraged  in some settings  i mean where does the consistency i  think people are grown up they  understand  that many people are frustrated with  wearing masks it's not particularly  pleasant experience if for example  you're on a long train journey  and if the carriage is empty uh then  people quite rightly then say well  is it necessary and that's what step  four and the vaccine enables us  to get rid of but at the same time if  you're on a very busy  commuter tube for example if you're with  people you don't frequently meet  what the guidance is saying is then it's  prudent to wear a mask but what we've  changed is the  legal requirement so we're saying it's  no longer the law to do so  but let's trust your viewers to reach  their judgment when it's crowded when  it's busy  of course we're a matter of two days ago  judgment  two days after july the 19th there will  be a session of prime minister's  questions in which  social distancing requirements in the  chamber will be removed you will be  cheated by gel with your colleagues  there is no  mandatory requirement for people to wear  face masks in the chamber and one must  suspect given what we've heard from  colleagues within your party that some  people will not be wearing them  i mean two days after the country's told  you know let's  stagger returning to work let's wear  masks when you're in crowded places  we're going to see politicians doing the  entire opposite  again i think it's right that parliament  uh the national pilot comes back  together we  we get that working as it should it  enables the government to be scrutinized  effectively it's for the speaker uh not  for members of the government to decide  what the rules are  that apply uh in the chamber but what  the guidance has said and what the chief  medical officer was very clear in the  press conference yesterday  is that people will form their judgments  in terms of when it's crowded  as to what is appropriate and i think  your viewers will get that we're  trusting british people  rather than making it a legal  requirement today  you cannot see what happened at wembley  way at the weekend there was no social  distancing  no one was wearing any masks those are  the people that you're asking to show  some personal responsibility  those are the people you're asking to  show some common sense you know if we've  got england fans  sticking flares up their backside it  does seem a bit of a  it does seem a bit of a stretch to say  that they're going to be taking the  personal response  i think when we're sharing exceptional  cases like that individual i don't think  is where  the british public are in terms of that  we're not winning well i think there  were some people hugely regrettably and  the wembley authorities were reviewing  those who broke into the stadium but  again i think we're talking about a very  small number  of people in a particular circumstance  so that is not the norm of the british  people  and the british people i think will  understand the distinction between the  legal requirement to wear mass  which has been the position up to this  point and as we learn to live with this  virus  as we move to step four as we benefit  from the british public responding  in the way they have to taking up the  vaccine that allows us to free up  uh the legal requirement and have  guidance and people will form their  judgment if they're meeting people who  are not from their usual family group  their friendship group  they're meeting people in a very crowded  place they will have the option then to  wear a mask  and that is what the gardeners will  recommend but but as the racism  directed at the players you know on the  pitch inside wembley demonstrates the  actions of a small minority  can have a huge impact i just want to  focus a little bit on the racism  that the footballers have have  experienced tyron mings  wrote this on twitter last night you  don't get to stoke the fire at the  beginning of the tournament by labeling  our anti-racism messages gesture  politics  and then pretend to be disgusted when  the very thing we're campaigning against  happens that's an england footballer a  black england footballer  laying some of the blame for the racism  that he and his colleagues experienced  at the door of your party at the door of  your home secretary well i  completely condemn the racism that those  footballers have suffered i think  the country at large does and has been  shot by it and that's why the government  is taking action through the online  harms bill the culture secretary  as you know has been meeting with senior  black footballers  uh working with them to ensure that the  the companies that provide the platforms  for this racist abuse are we're bringing  legislation  forward to do that what has been taken  against natalie elfic  your colleague within the party who sent  a message to the conservatives whatapp  group  they lost would it be ungenerous to  suggest rashford should have spent more  time  perfecting his game and less time  playing politics i mean what does it say  about the state of your party  that she felt comfortable sending that  message to our colleagues well that  message was wrong and natalie has  apologized  for it but we're taking action to tackle  at this  through the online homes bill and  that is what needs to happen we need to  ensure that those platforms  take action and if they don't the  government will take action against them  i have often quoted the prime minister  to government ministers on occasion such  as this his comments about  pikanini's with watermelon smiles the  problem with africa is that we're not in  charge anymore  his comments about muslim women looking  about like letterboxes and the response  is always  that they need to be viewed in context  but isn't the context that those remarks  now need to be seen in  is that the dog's abuse that has been  thrown against  black english footballers simply because  they missed a penalty kick  isn't that the context in which we need  to view those remarks now no we need to  condemn those remarks  uh against the footballers the the the  racism that they've suffered is  completely  unacceptable that is why uh we are  taking action  in terms of the online's harm bill and  that is why the platforms  that provide the opportunity for people  to make those remarks  themselves need to take urgent action  we've been very clear on that the  culture secretary  has been working with the fa uh with  leading footballers  uh to ensure action is taken is  completely unacceptable  and 